# Accelerometer orientation for iio-sensor-proxy (and so for KDE/GNOME
# auto-rotate), both models. The compositor turns the output by the panel's
# mount (the landscape panel sits at 270 degrees) and the sensor orientation
# on top, so the accelerometer has to be given in the panel's frame: upright
# on the keyboard stand is "normal". SMGR reports north-east-down axes and
# +1 g upwards; this matrix is a rotation into the panel's frame, so the
# tilt (face up or down) comes out right too. On an SM-T820 on the keyboard
# stand it gives normal and tilted-up (the SMGR driver's own matrix gives
# right-up there); on an SM-T825 the same X and Y were confirmed in all
# positions (with Z +1, which turned only the tilt over).
SUBSYSTEM=="iio", ATTR{name}=="qcom-smgr-accel", ENV{ACCEL_MOUNT_MATRIX}="1, 0, 0; 0, -1, 0; 0, 0, -1"

# Stream the accelerometer at its lowest rate, about 12.5 Hz, not its fastest
# (416 Hz). iio-sensor-proxy reads it every 700 ms and uses only the newest
# sample, and each sample is a QMI message that wakes a CPU: on the SM-T820
# with the screen on, 416 Hz cost about 385 wakeups a second and 2 % of the
# CPU time. The rate takes effect with kernel patch 0143.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="iio", ATTR{name}=="qcom-smgr-accel", ATTR{in_accel_sampling_frequency}="12"
